{
  "title": "Normalize: one registry of recipe operations, re-runnable in any combination (rename enrich -> normalize)",
  "description": "Today the things we can do to a recipe after (or during) import are a pile of special cases: the scrape pipeline's core steps plus ScrapeAutoAppliedAiEnrichment (ramekin-core/src/pipeline/auto_enrichments.rs) for title/description/tags; separate endpoints for POST /api/recipes/{id}/normalize-title, /generate-description, /rescrape, /rescrape-photo, /generate-photo, POST /api/enrich (which despite the name only suggests tags), and POST /api/recipes/reparse-ingredients (all recipes, skips hand-edited ones); and each client wires its own menu item, progress state and polling for each one (ramekin-ios/Ramekin/RecipeDetailView.swift menu, RecipeDetailViewModel+Enrichment.swift, ramekin-ui/src/utils/aiEnrichments.ts, CookbookBulkToolbar.tsx). \
They are all one idea: a recipe version is derived from a source by applying operations, and any subset can be re-applied at any time. \
Proposed model (confirm with the owner before building): (1) Source operations, which replace the base content and so come first: `refetch` (download the source URL again, storing a new capture) and `reextract` (rebuild the recipe from the stored capture with the current extractor and ingredient parser; for text/photo imports, from the stored original text or photos). `refetch` implies `reextract`. (2) Normalize operations, transforms over the current content: normalize title, generate description, auto-tag, resolve ingredient names, and later house style (see normalize-3). \
AI photo generation and photo rescrape can be ops too, in their own group. (3) One registry in ramekin-core where each op declares its id, label, group, whether it calls AI, which fields it writes, and which ops it must run after (description reads the normalized title, as reads_enrichments does today). \
The scrape pipeline becomes 'source ops + the user's default normalize ops' built from that registry, and `make pipeline` runs the same registry. (4) One endpoint, e.g. POST /api/recipes/{id}/normalize {ops: [...]} (and a bulk form taking recipe ids), that orders the ops by the registry, runs them as a job on the existing scrape-job/step machinery, and saves one new version recording which ops produced it (version source 'normalize' plus the op list), so history shows what ran. \
A GET endpoint lists the ops so neither client hard-codes the list (see doc/client-logic-sharing.md). \
End-to-end tests before either client uses them. (5) Hand edits: a source op discards hand edits made since the last source-derived version. \
Today reparse-all silently skips those recipes; instead the job reports that the recipe has hand edits so the client can warn (the sheet in normalize-2) and the user decides. \
Open question for the owner: should normalize results save straight away (version history is the undo) or come back as a preview to accept, like EnrichPreviewSheet does today? \
Recommendation: save straight away for single recipes and show the version diff afterwards (RecipeVersionCompareSheet), since every run is a new version anyway. \
Rename 'enrich' to 'normalize' throughout as part of this: core step names (enrich_normalize_title etc.), the scrape job 'enriching' status, API paths and types (CustomEnrichRequest stays with the customize work, see customize-1), and client code. \
No compatibility shims are needed. \
Remove the old per-op endpoints and reparse-all once both clients use the new one. \
Step renames change pipeline output paths, so expect intentional data/ diffs from `make pipeline`; output should otherwise be unchanged.",
